\par\par \b \uc2  IMF Sees Chile's GDP Growth At 2.6% For 2002 \b0\par\par\uc2 386 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Juli 2002\par 17:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON -(Dow Jones)- Thanks to "sound and consistent" economic policies, the outlook for Chile remains bright despite the financial troubles of its neighbors, the International Monetary Fund said Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  While others in South America are facing recession this year, the IMF predicts Chile will post gross domestic  product growth of 2.6%, a forecast on the conservative side of market expectations. \par \par \uc2  "This performance, in the face of an adverse external environment, is also a reflection of Chile's political stability, good governance, and credible institutions, which serve to reinforce the economic agenda of the authorities," the IMF said in a press summary of the IMF's  annual "Article IV" review of Chile's economy. \par \par \uc2  Chile's inflation-targeting framework combined with its floating exchange rate regime continues to serve well,  the IMF said. Considering the output gap and subdued inflation, the Fund expressed approval for recent reductions in interest rates by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Fund  also lauded \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy of using \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow movements in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, rather  than to prop up an exchange-rate target. \par \par \uc2  The IMF said the results of staff tests, and indicators like prudential and earnings ratios, point to a   sound banking system. Some IMF directors urged Chile to aim for increased bank competition to help lower interest rates, particularly for small business loans. \par \par \uc2  The Fund did caution that its favorable forecast for Chile assumes that domestic demand will strengthen fairly soon in response to the monetary-policy easing. The slowdown in GDP growth from rates above 9% in the late 1990s is probably mostly the result of temporary factors, the IMF said. Still, the government should continue to boost economic activity through its ongoing program of market-friendly reforms, including development of a venture capital market, and improving regulation and the legal framework. \par \par \uc2  The government must work harder to reduce Chile's stubbornly high rates of unemployment, estimated above 9% for 2001 and well above the 6% rate seen before the 1999 recession. \par \par \uc2  The IMF "suggested that greater attention be placed on  education and job training for low-skilled workers and on  other ways of enhancing labor market flexibility more generally." \par \par \uc2  -By Elizabeth Price, Dow Jones Newswires; 202-862-9295; Elizabeth.Price@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020020731dy7v0073l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
