\par\par \b \uc2  Chile's Peso Ends Weaker; Still Shy Of 700/Dlr Mark \b0\par\par\uc2 183 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Oktober 2001\par 20:05\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SANTIAGO -(Dow Jones)- Chile's peso finished weaker Monday, ending official trade at CLP695.70 to the dollar compared with Friday's CLP693.00 close. \par \par \uc2  Traders said activity was thin, some adding that market players were hesitant about another possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has repeatedly pumped dollars into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in recent weeks, after announcing on Aug. 16 plans to sell up to $2 billion in reserves by year-end to shore up the peso's decline. \par \par \uc2  "Based on the signals, the bank seems to be coming out automatically when the peso approaches CLP697 (to the dollar)," said one local trader with a foreign bank. The peso traded in the CLP695-range all of last week. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has confirmed eight market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  since mid-August. According to traders, the bank injects only small quantities of dollars into the market in any given session, typically less than $50 million. \par \par \uc2  -Andrea Welsh, Dow Jones Newswires; 562-460-8547; chile@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1170 1844 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011001dxa10011p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
