\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Chile \highlight\uc2  Peso Firming, Traders Cite Ctrl Bank  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 236 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2001\par 17:46\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SANTIAGO -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso was firming against the dollar midsession Monday, after falling nearly to the 700-level early in the session. \par \par \uc2  Local traders attributed the rebound to the stronger than expected performance of U.S. and regional equities markets bolstered by a surprise \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  At 1530 GMT, traders quoted the peso at CLP688.50 against the dollar, compared to Friday's CLP690.30 close. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had already rallied to CLP694.50 before the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped in. \par \par \uc2  Local traders said the peso plunged at the open on fears that U.S. equities might tank when markets reopened Monday after last week's closure. The peso quickly began to firm, they said, when the Dow Jones Industrial Average appeared to stabilize. At 1540 GMT, the DJIA was down 4.9%, fighting its way back from a more than 6% decline. \par \par \uc2  Rising regional markets were also supporting the peso, said a trader for Chile's Banco de Estado. At 1540, Brazil's Bovespa Index was up 4.3%, while Argentina's Merval was ahead 2.6%. \par \par \uc2  A surprise 50 basis point rate cut handed down by the Federal Reserve should lend support to the peso as the market begins to calm, said Roberto Guzman of the Covarrubias Y Cia brokerage. \par \par \uc2  -Andrea Welsh, Dow Jones Newswires; 562-460-8547; chile@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1170 1844 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010917dx9h02j64\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw