\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Chile \highlight\uc2 's Ctrl Bank  \highlight22\uc2 Intervenes \highlight\uc2  In  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Market - Traders \b0\par\par\uc2 579 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 September 2001\par 19:08\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SANTIAGO -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold dollars directly on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market shortly before the close of Thursday's session, traders said, leading peso to end 0.5% stronger at CLP664.30 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed Wednesday at CLP667.50 a dollar. \par \par \uc2  The peso was trading at CLP667.30 at 1630 GMT, right before the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . By 1645 GMT, the peso had jumped to CLP664.50 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The total amount of dollars that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold on the market was not known, though one local trader at an international bank estimated that it was about $30 million. \par \par \uc2  Thursday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  marks the third time in two weeks that the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  has entered into the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market after announcing its willingness to tap foreign reserves to shore up the peso in mid-August. \par \par \uc2  The bank first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Aug. 28 when the peso hit CLP666.00, and then again on Aug. 31 when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had weakened to CLP663.00. \par \par \uc2  On both occasions, traders said that the amount that the bank sold on the market didn't surpass $50 million. The \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in turn, has only directed markets to its biweekly foreign reserves report, due out Friday. \par \par \uc2  The peso on Thursday opened official trading at CLP668.00, but then strengthened slightly to trade between CLP666.00-CLP667.00 on corporate sector dollar sales, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's hit its intraday strong point of CLP666.00 at 1445 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the bank's monetary policy board meeting, later Thursday, was having no effect on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  because markets expect local interest rates to remain at their current nominal 6.50%. Nor do traders expect the publication of the next schedule of dollar-denominated bond auctions, due out Friday, to have much of an impact. \par \par \uc2  Besides \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly in the market, the bank is also selling dollar-denominated notes, or PRDs, to provide the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  with additional cushion. As of Thursday's auction, the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have sold about $1.4 billion in the notes in two months. \par \par \uc2  -By Christen A. Jamar, Dow Jones Newswires; 562-460-8546; chile@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--Chile's peso ended official trading on Friday at 666.90 pesos to the dollar, practically wiping out Thursday's 0.5% gain on a \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold dollars directly on the market when the peso hit around CLP667.00, leading it to close the previous session at CLP664.30 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Traders said Friday that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell back on Friday on a lack of dollar liquidity on the market. Total volumes on the spot market amounted to a thin $260 million, approximately. \par \par \uc2  Market players are holding on to their U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings on the seemingly constant risk that neighboring Argentina could still dive into even deeper financial risk despite increased funding from multilateral lenders. \par \par \uc2  As a result, one trader said, the only market player really willing to sell dollars is the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Adding a little bit more pressure to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the Bank's decision on Thursday to maintain local interest rates at 6.50%, traders said. While markets expected rates to stay unchanged, there was some speculation that there could be a rate hike. \par \par \uc2  -By Christen A. Jamar, Dow Jones Newswires; 562-460-8546; chile@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1170 1844 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010906dx9600138\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
