\par\par \b \uc2  SURVEY: Chile Aug CPI Seen +0.4%;  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Effect Weighed \b0\par\par\uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 656 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 September 2001\par 21:50\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Consumer Price Index:\par            Aug     July   June    Aug/00    12-Mo/00\par Forecast  +0.4%   +0.0%   +0.1%   +0.3%       ---\par Actual     ---    -0.2%   +0.1%   +0.3%      +4.5%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Christen A. Jamar\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  SANTIAGO (Dow Jones)--Chile's consumer price index likely climbed in August, driven principally by steady increases in fuel prices during the month. \par \par \uc2  While the fuel price effect has become a familiar story, markets will be closely watching the government's report - due out Wednesday at 1430 GMT - to see if the country's sharply weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has finally started to trickle into consumer prices. \par \par \uc2  A Dow Jones Newswires survey of seven economists yielded a forecast of 0.4% inflation for August, after 0.2% deflation in July. \par \par \uc2  State-owned petroleum refiner ENAP implemented a series of hikes during August, translating into higher transportation costs and, to a lesser degree, housing costs. ENAP, which imports or produces about 90% of the fuel used in Chile, sets fuel prices to distributors on a weekly basis. \par \par \uc2  For much of the past year, the CPI has fluctuated in line with fuel prices and their subsequent affect on transportation costs. \par \par \uc2  Inflation in most other sectors, meanwhile, has stayed stable as Chile's depressed consumer spending environment prevented businesses from hiking prices. \par \par \uc2  That has held true even as the peso lost as much of 17% of its value against the dollar this year on concerns about Argentina and weak copper prices. \par \par \uc2  But economists say the softer \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  might have led to "marginal" price increases in some areas of the economy during August. \par \par \uc2  Specifically, prices for some food products and housing appliances might have seen small incremental hikes. There's also a possibility that the weaker peso has led clothing prices higher, although that's seen offset by end-of-season sales. \par \par \uc2  If August's inflation comes in higher than expected, the focus is going to quickly shift to the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policy board monthly meeting, set for Thursday, as some economists banter around the idea of an interest rate hike. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's year-end target range for inflation is between 2% and 4%. Consumer prices were up 1.3% over the first seven months of the year. At its last meeting, in early August, the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  left rates unchanged at a nominal 6.50%. \par \par \uc2  Chile's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced in mid-August a two-tier attack on the falling peso, signaling to markets its concern about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's effect on inflation. \par \par \uc2  It said it would sell up to $2 billion more in dollar-denominated bonds, or PRDs, starting next week and through the end of the year, possibly bringing the total amount of the notes in circulation to about $4.5 billion. \par \par \uc2  But far more symbolic, economists say, is the bank's announcement that it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly on the market. \par \par \uc2  Last Tuesday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold dollars on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the first time since it floated the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  two years ago. It \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  a second time on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "The only interpretation that can be made is that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is worried about the effect of the (weaker peso) on inflation," say Universidad Gabriela Mistral economist Erick Haindl. \par \par \uc2  Still, many economists say a rate hike is still remote. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can't give a negative signal," says Haindl, adding that the bank is likely to stick with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rather than risk \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's nascent recovery in demand. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders also said Tuesday that the market is expecting the bank to maintain rates on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The peso closed Tuesday's session at 664.70 to the dollar, 3.7% stronger than when the Bank announced its willingness to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly in the market. \par \par \uc2  -Christen A. Jamar, Dow Jones Newswires; 562-460-8547; chile@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010904dx9400171\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
