\par\par \b \uc2  Chile  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  sells dollars to prop up peso. \b0\par\par\uc2 168 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 August 2001\par 17:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SANTIAGO, \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Aug 31 (Reuters) - The Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, selling dollars to defend the peso for the second time this week, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The influx of an unspecified amount of dollars into the market firmed the peso, which was trading at 660.50/661.50 pesos to the U.S. dollar at mid-session compared with Thursday's closing rate of 661.70/662.00. \par \par \uc2  The Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has depreciated about 15 percent since the start of the year because of fears of a debt default or devaluation in neighboring Argentina, Latin America's third-largest economy. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  injected an estimated $14 million into the market, its first direct involvement in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate since 1999, when it floated the peso. \par \par \uc2  The institution announced on Aug. 16 it would withdraw up to $2 billion from its reserves in the remainder of 2001 to pump into the market. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010831dx8v00q93\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
