\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 3-Chile  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  sells dollars to bolster peso. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Froilan Romero \par \uc2 426 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 August 2001\par 01:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  (Adds \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  statement and estimated amount of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) \par \par \uc2  SANTIAGO, \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Aug 28 (Reuters) - The Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, selling dollars for the first time since 1999 to prop up the peso , which has sunk to record lows amid Argentina's financial woes, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The peso had buckled early in Tuesday's session under heavy demand for dollars by banks, hitting a low of 666.70 pesos to the U.S. dollar. It was the lowest rate of the session, but the peso has recovered somewhat from its record low close at 691.70 registered on Aug. 14. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened immediately as a result of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  and closed at 658.00/658.50, up 0.92 percent from Monday's close of 664.10/664.40. \par \par \uc2  After markets closed, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed that it pumped money into the market but did not disclose the amount. \par \par \uc2  "Today, on August 28, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  carried out \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sale \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the interbank market," it said in a brief statement, adding that it would report changes in its foreign reserves in a routine September 7 report. \par \par \uc2  Chile's foreign reserves stood at $14.473 billion at the end of July. \par \par \uc2  Traders in 20 banks surveyed by Reuters estimated that the institution sold about $14 million overall, saying they considered it a small-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but with a strong impact. \par \par \uc2  The Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not disclose details of its market transactions, but earlier this month it said it would inject up to $2 billion into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market during the remainder of this year, drawing from reserves. \par \par \uc2  It also had announced previously it would increase the amount of debt it issues in the form of dollar-denominated promissory notes by up to $2 billion, putting a ceiling of $4.5 billion on the issue of these notes by the end of 2001. \par \par \uc2  The institution had refrained from direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since 1999, when it abandoned an exchange-rate band regime and floated the peso. \par \par \uc2  The Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has depreciated about 15 percent since the start of the year amid widespread fears of a debt default or devaluation in neighboring Argentina, Latin America's third-largest economy. \par \par \uc2  The peso had a three-day rally last week - gaining 2.6 percent - after Argentina struck a deal for an $8 billion loan from the IMF, but began to lose ground on Monday. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010828dx8s00yq9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
