\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Chile \highlight\uc2 's Peso Ends Firmer, 658.00/Dlr; Ctrl Bk  \highlight22\uc2 Intervenes \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 349 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 August 2001\par 19:08\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SANTIAGO -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso ended stronger at CLP658.00 to the dollar Tuesday, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the first time since floating the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  two years ago. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  warned two weeks ago that it was prepared to spend up to $2 billion in reserves to defend the peso. Local traders weren't able to say immediately how many dollars the bank pumped into the market Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The bank began selling dollars around midsession, traders reported, after corporate demand and rising speculation pressured the peso to a weak point of CLP666.60 against the dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had closed Monday at CLP664.00. \par \par \uc2  Before this week, Chile's peso had gained 4.4% over seven sessions, an impressive rally after fears of an Argentine debt default pushed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a record low of CLP691.70 per dollar on Aug. 14. \par \par \uc2  Traders and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts attributed the rebound to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  warning, together with news that the International Monetary Fund will tack another $8 billion onto an emergency credit line for Argentina. \par \par \uc2  Chile's peso gained steadily on Argentina's good news last week, despite rising doubts about whether the IMF aid will be enough to help Argentina avoid default. \par \par \uc2  Two hours after official trading in Chile's peso closed Tuesday, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders were still unclear about the extent of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Several traders said spot market volumes were above average during the session, totaling about $700 million to $800 million. Given that daily volumes have typically averaged nearer the $500-million mark in recent months, traders deduced that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold a limited amount of dollars on the spot market. \par \par \uc2  One trader reported talk that the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold as little as $30 million. The sale triggered a swift reaction from market players, he said, adding that foreign banks were active during the session. \par \par \uc2  -Andrea Welsh, Dow Jones Newswires; 562-460-8547; chile@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1170 1844 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010828dx8s0010k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
