\par\par \b \uc2  Chile's Peso Ends 0.6% Stronger At CLP676.00/Dlr \b0\par\par\uc2 322 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 2001\par 19:07\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SANTIAGO -(Dow Jones)- Chile's peso firmed 0.6% against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday, ending the session at CLP676.00 per dollar compared with Friday's CLP680.00 close. \par \par \uc2  The peso opened moderately stronger at CLP678.30, but then leaped nearly 0.8% in the last hour of trading, climbing from just above the 678-level to CLP673.00 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  Local traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  put in a request to price dollars, immediately sparking rumors that the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  might step in to sell some of its U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves into the market. \par \par \uc2  The Bank announced late last week that it had set aside $2 billion in reserves for possible \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  between now and the end of the year. \par \par \uc2  The peso then softened in the last few minutes of the session, easing back to CLP676.00 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  At the end of Monday's session, traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  hadn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  "Most likely, they did it just to startle (market participants)," one trader commented. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't directly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since adopting a floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy two years ago. The bank did, however, launch a program to sell dollar-indexed bonds into the market after the peso began to drop sharply in early July. \par \par \uc2  The bank announced last week that it was prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly, after watching the peso sink to a record low of CLP691.70 against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Falling prices for Chilean commodity exports together with fears that Argentina's economic troubles will spark a regional financial crisis have pressured the peso to depreciate up to 17% against the dollar this year. \par \par \uc2  At Monday's close, the peso was off 15% against the dollar from Jan. 1. \par \par \uc2  -By Andrea Welsh, Dow Jones Newswires; 562-460-8547; chile@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1170 1844 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010820dx8k003x1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
