\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Chile \highlight\uc2  cenbank  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2  to boost peso. \b0\par\par\uc2 341 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 2001\par 03:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SANTIAGO, \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Aug 16 (Reuters) - The Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday announced it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to prop up the beleaguered peso, which has fallen to record lows recently mainly because of financial woes in Argentina. \par \par \uc2  In a statement released after markets closed, the institution said it would withdraw up to $2 billion from its foreign reserves to sell dollars directly in the market over the remainder of this year. \par \par \uc2  This marks the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's first direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the exchange rate since it floated the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1999. \par \par \uc2  It also announced it would increase its placement of dollar-denominated promissory notes by up to $2 billion above previously announced amounts, putting a ceiling of $4.5 billion on the issue of these notes by the end of this year. \par \par \uc2  "The objective (of the measures) is to give greater stability to financial markets in the country in a scenario in which negative external conditions persist," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said, referring to Argentina. \par \par \uc2  The Chilean peso firmed slightly on Thursday to close at 689.20/689.50 pesos to the U.S. dollar on market speculation of a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is autonomous but works closely with the finance ministry, first stepped in to break the peso's fall in early July, announcing an extra $1 billion in dollar-denominated promissory notes over the next six months to help banks hedge against the declining \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The move temporarily bolstered the peso, which was quickly swept down again by chronic fears of a debt default in Argentina, Latin America's third-largest economy. \par \par \uc2  The latest \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  move comes amid intense market speculation that Argentina may be on the verge of securing up to $9 billion in fresh loans from the International Monetary Fund, supplementing a $40 billion multi-lender rescue package already approved. \par \par \uc2  After seven days of loan negotiations in Washington, Argentine government officials and the IMF have given no hints about the possible outcome of the talks. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010817dx8h0020i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
