\par\par \b \uc2  Chile's Peso Ends At 664.00/Dlr, Bounces Back From 669.50 \b0\par\par\uc2 388 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juli 2001\par 19:22\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SANTIAGO -(Dow Jones)- Chile's peso ended at a record weak point of CLP664.00 against the dollar Wednesday, after fighting its way back from an historic intraday low of CLP669.50 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The peso plunged early, in line with other emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as market players sought out dollars to hedge against a burgeoning crisis set off in part by economic troubles in Argentina, one of Chile's closest neighbors. \par \par \uc2  Traders reported that the heavy covering activity in Chile kicked off early Wednesday on Tuesday's report that Argentina had auctioned Treasury bills at a 14.01% annual rate. \par \par \uc2  The higher than expected rate reignited fears that Chile's neighbor will be forced to devaluate its own dollar-pegged \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  or default on public debt, exacerbating regional dollar outflows. \par \par \uc2  "Corporate (players) and financial holdings can cover easily here given the lower financing costs in Chile," said Francisco Sanchez, a local trader with Hellernet Sud. \par \par \uc2  However, the peso bounced back after approaching the 670-mark as lack of direction ahead of the coming session led to some moderate profit-taking. \par \par \uc2  "The market's confused because it doesn't depend only on us," said BanEdwards trader Cristian Donoso, citing broad uncertainty about how emerging markets, particularly Argentina, might open up Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Many market players also suspect \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might directly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market going forward, Donoso said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced last week that it will sell $1 billion in dollar-denominated bonds, or PRDs, into local financial markets by the end of the year. The Bank auctioned the first $100 million tranche Tuesday, and is set to sell another $100 million Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Talk is beginning to spread that the Bank might dip into its reserves to pump dollars directly into the spot market to prop up the peso, as the planned PRD auctions appear to have failed to fully offset external pressures on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "(The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors) made a pretty overt statement...and now they've lost credibility," said FleetBoston \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst Greg Anderson. "It hurts to potentially lose reserves, but on the other hand the dollar-indexed debt didn't work." \par \par \uc2  -Andrea Welsh, Dow Jones Newswires; 562-460-8547; chile@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1170 1844 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx7b033ot\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
