\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Argentine stocks end at lows unseen since early 1999. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Gilbert Le Gras \par \uc2 634 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Juli 2001\par 23:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BUENOS AIRES, Argentina, July 4 (Reuters) - Argentine shares edged down on Wednesday, sinking to their lowest levels since Brazil devalued its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1999, in the wake of presidential resignation rumors on Tuesday, traders said. \par \par \uc2  In an interview with La Nacion newspaper, President Fernando de la Rua dismissed as "nonsense" talk that he was "overwhelmed" and about to quit as president of Argentina. \par \par \uc2  The center-left coalition leader said the rumors were spread by people "speculating against the country." \par \par \uc2  The benchmark MerVal dropped 0.80 percent to close at 385.96 points, near its lowest point since Brazil's steep devaluation in mid-January 1999. \par \par \uc2  The MerVal touched a low of 384.98 earlier in the session which was marked by anemic volume of $6.4 million. Decliners tripled advancers 18 to six while six issues were unchanged. \par \par \uc2  Frustration with the torpor that has afflicted Latin America's third-largest economy for the last three years is reaching new heights. \par \par \uc2  A member of De la Rua's own party, Chaco provincial Gov. Angel Rozas on Tuesday said the president was "overwhelmed" by the ongoing crisis, which fueled talk the 63-year-old leader, treated last month for arteriosclerosis, might step down. \par \par \uc2  "No one would seriously pull out of an investment based on rumors. I think yesterday's rumors were an expression of desire by investors for what they'd like to see done," said Leopoldo Olivario of Bacque brokerage. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Brazil and \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2  tumbled on Argentine woes and Uruguay's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the fourth consecutive business day to defend its battered money. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Argentina's top trade partner, Brazil's real , closed seven cents lower at 2.42 reais per U.S. dollar, a 2.85 percent depreciation from Tuesday's close and a level not seen since Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced aggressive plans to prop up that money on June 21. \par \par \uc2  Argentina's country risk rating - the spread between Argentine bonds and safe-haven U.S. Treasuries - was up 17 basis points at 1,094 basis points. \par \par \uc2  "The market will continue to trend down as long as we have political noise which will lead to expectations the government will have to pay high interest rates at the next Letes (Treasury bill) auction," said Luis Corsiglia of Corsiglia y Cia. brokerage. \par \par \uc2  The next Letes T-bill auction is scheduled for July 10 when $500 million in 364-day T-bills and $350 million in 182-day T-bills will be sold. \par \par \uc2  Another factor that has unnerved foreign investors is an ongoing spat between opposition Peronist Party provincial governors who complain the central government owes them about $225 million in transfer funds for social and other programs. \par \par \uc2  De la Rua's government negotiated the deal last November in exchange for a commitment by the provinces to freeze their primary public spending at 2000 levels for five years. \par \par \uc2  The government Tuesday night failed to reach an accord with opposition Peronist Party provincial governors, who control most of Argentina's 23 provinces, on the disbursement of social program and other funds. \par \par \uc2  Argentine stocks and bonds have suffered most of the year amid concern over Argentina's ability to pay its $128 billion debt during an economic slump verging on three years. \par \par \uc2  Investors want to see positive numbers in economic indicators such as tax collection that show that pro-competition, pro-growth measures announced by Economy Minister Domingo Cavallo over the last three months are actually working to pull the Argentine economy out of its lull. \par \par \uc2  Investors have said that only with sustained growth will Argentina be able to meet its debt obligations. U.S. markets, meanwhile, were closed for the U.S. Independence Day holiday. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010912dx7400901\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
