\par\par \b \uc2  Chile foreign reserves seen stable in 98-Santander. \b0\par\par\uc2 189 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Mai 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SANTIAGO, May 7 (Reuters) - Chile's foreign reserves should remain stable in 1998 despite recent dollar sales designed to defend the peso, Santander Investment said Thursday in a report. \par \par \uc2  "Losses likely will be reduced in coming months, as authorities are favoring the reduction of local liquidity, thus increasing short-term interest rates temporarily," Santander said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the peso in several \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading sessions this year. Foreign reserve levels have fluctuated as a result. \par \par \uc2  Earlier Thursday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Chile's foreign reserves dropped $21 million in April to end the month at $16.915 billion. \par \par \uc2  "Given that the decline in copper prices is the main factor behind the fall in terms of trade and (given that) prices are expected to slide even further during 1999 (to 0.72 cents a lb, the exchange rate should depreciate to 470 per dollar by end-1998 from 439.81 at end-1997 and to 484 by end-1999." it added. ((Chris Aspin, Santiago newsroom +562 699-5595 ext 211)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du5705q64\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
