\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS - Chile raises rates after peso reels. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Axel Bugge \par \uc2 714 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Januar 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SANTIAGO, Jan 8 (Reuters) - Chile's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised rates Thursday after financial markets reeled on mounting concerns Latin America's star economy could be in for a rough ride in 1998 on contagion from Asia's financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  "This decision was taken due the potential impact of the new international situation," the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in a statement referring to the Asian financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  "This new situation has made it necessary to moderate the evolution of aggregate demand to lower its affect on the current account and inflation." \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced late Thursday a rise in its interbank lending rate target to 7.0 percent from 6.5 percent. \par \par \uc2  The move followed an acceleration of the week's trend  Thursday in the pesos' losses against the dollar. Investors are worried about the growing likelihood that the country's exports to Asia will be hit hard as a result of the financial crisis, causing an economic slow-down in Chile. \par \par \uc2  The peso closed at 463.50 per dollar, way below Wednesday's close of 450. At the end of last week the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stood at 440. \par \par \uc2  There have also been concerns that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be unable to meet this year's inflation target of 4.5 percent on the back of imported inflation from the weaker peso. Last year's inflation came in at 6.0 percent, overshooting the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 5.5 percent target. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said together with the finance minister that it will continue to monitor the development of the economy at home and in international markets in order to, "adopt the necessary measures of sustaining the macroeconomic targets." \par \par \uc2  Analysts' responses were mixed. \par \par \uc2  "I see it as taking an aspirin...it does not contribute to resolving the root of the problem," said Henry Rudnick, manager of the CB Capitales consultancy. \par \par \uc2  "The measure also implies a recognition that country risk has increased on the external situation." \par \par \uc2  Tomas Flores, an economist at the Liberty and Development Institute said that, with the rate increase, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will meet its inflation target. \par \par \uc2  The market slide this week has uncovered concerns about Chile's economy. As one of Latin America's biggest traders and with its similarities to Asia's little tigers, its high savings rates, aggressive exports and sustained growth over more than a decade, Chile could now be directly hit from the Asian fall-out. \par \par \uc2  About $5.3 billion of its total $17 billion in exports is expected, in 1997, to go to Asia. And the price of copper, the country's main export, has ratcheted lower amid the commodities slump induced by Asia's crisis. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Eduardo Aninat on Wednesday urged moderation in economic policy, but said, "\b \highlight22\uc2 Chile\b0 \highlight\uc2  can resist this crisis perfectly well." \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Wednesday and Monday to smooth the pesos' fall, but did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market continues to show strong demand for dollars, which has not been halted by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said one trader. \par \par \uc2  "In reality, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has shown it does not have sufficient force to prevent the rise of the dollar." \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has about $17 billion in foreign reserves. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the continued performance of the peso will depend on how markets approve of the rate increase, but also on whether or not additional measures need to be taken. \par \par \uc2  One way to curb the pesos' drop would be to lower restrictions on the amount of foreign capital that can be taken into the country. Those restrictions, which aim to curb speculation, require foreigners to deposit 30 percent of their capital with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a year in a non-interest earning account. An easing of those restrictions would allow more dollars to flow into the economy, boosting demand for the peso. \par \par \uc2  As the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said, the current situation is compounded by the current account deficit, which is seen rising this year on lower raw materials exports. The government has raised its estimate for the 1998 current account deficit to 5.0 percent of gross domestic product from 4.5 percent. ((Santiago Newsroom, 562 699-5595)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1998 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du18097tm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
